For this source request, return one root object containing exactly two
properties: "term_results" and "source_context_translations". Both properties
are JSON objects. Never return a root "cards", "contextual_cards", or
"additional_sense_cards" array.

TERM RESULTS

"term_results" is keyed by the exact decimal rank strings required by the
response schema. For every object in the input "words" array, use its numeric
"rank" as a JSON property name (for example, input rank 7 becomes property
"7"). Return every required rank exactly once, add no other rank, and do not
put a "rank" field inside its value.

Every rank value contains exactly:

- "contextual_sense": one object describing only the sense and grammatical
  role used by that ranked source occurrence.
- "additional_senses": an array containing only genuinely disjoint additional
  common lexical senses for the same ranked term. Return an empty array when
  there is no qualifying additional sense.

The strict schema lists the requested lexical fields inside each sense object.
It deliberately excludes the source-language term field because the processor
restores the exact input term from its rank. Do not copy the term into either
"contextual_sense" or an "additional_senses" item. This source-specific rule
overrides any general instruction to copy a supplied term into a term field.

"contextual_sense" also excludes "Sentences" and
"Sentence Translations (English)". The processor inserts the retained source
context and its matching translation for this one sense. Do not repeat,
rewrite, delimit, or repeat either value in "contextual_sense".

Each input word supplies an "occurrence_span", a pair of zero-based character
offsets [start, end) relative to the referenced context object's "text". It
also supplies an "occurrence_locator":

- "target" is the exact selected occurrence.
- "before" and "after" are its immediately adjacent source excerpts.
- "literal_match_ordinal" is the 1-based, left-to-right ordinal of this exact
  spelling inside the full retained context.
- "marked_excerpt" places the selected occurrence between literal
  ⟪TARGET⟫ and ⟪/TARGET⟫ markers.

The "contextual_sense" object MUST describe the grammatical role and lexical
sense of the occurrence inside those markers. Never assign it the sense of
another identically spelled occurrence. Use before + target + after, the
marked excerpt, and the full referenced context together to verify the
selection; the numeric occurrence_span remains authoritative.

Before returning an empty "additional_senses" array, inspect every other
occurrence of the same spelling in the retained context. If another occurrence
plainly uses a different lexical sense from the marked target, that different
sense MUST be an "additional_senses" item. Also include every other genuinely
disjoint common sense required by the general source-batch rule, without
repeating the contextual sense.

Perform an explicit coverage audit for every rank before returning it. The
source title, author, genre, and selected passage identify the contextual sense;
they do not restrict additional senses to meanings used in that one work.
"Relevant language, period, and register" means the broader language a learner
will encounter in that historical setting. Check separately for a common
concrete or literal sense, a common verbal or grammatical use, and a common
abstract, institutional, or figurative sense. Include each category only when
it is genuinely lexical and disjoint, but do not collapse two such senses merely
because they share an origin. For example, Classical Chinese 道 as a concrete
road or path, 道 as a practical method/course/teaching in human affairs, 道 as
the metaphysical Way, and 道 as the verb to speak or state are disjoint senses
when they are common in the selected period. If one is contextual, the other
qualifying senses still belong in "additional_senses".

Every "additional_senses" item contains the same requested lexical fields as
"contextual_sense", plus "Sentences" as exactly three array items and
"Sentence Translations (English)" as exactly three positionally matched array
items. It contains no source-language term field. Follow all general generated
example and translation rules for these arrays.

SOURCE CONTEXT TRANSLATIONS

"source_context_translations" is keyed by the exact "context_id" strings
required by the response schema. For every object in the input "contexts"
array, copy its "context_id" character-for-character as one JSON property name.
Return every required context ID exactly once and add no other property.

The value of each context-ID property is one complete, natural English
translation of that entire context, directly as a JSON string. It is not an
object or array. Translate each context exactly once regardless of how many
ranks refer to it. The string may contain normal English punctuation and
multiple clauses or sentences, but never |, or any HTML.
Do not split one context translation into several values. If the context is
already Modern English, repeat it as plain text.
